---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import PuzzleTool from "../tools/PuzzleTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="puzzle">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        A jigsaw at any size and any number of pieces, with knobs that
        <strong class="text-slate-300">actually undercut</strong> — so the pieces hold each other rather than falling
        apart the moment you pick the puzzle up. Every joint is cut once, and the seed means the puzzle you liked is
        the puzzle you get back.
    </Fragment>

    <PuzzleTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </PuzzleTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The undercut is the whole thing</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    What makes a jigsaw a jigsaw is that the knob's <strong class="text-slate-300">neck is narrower
                    than its head</strong>. A piece can be lifted out of the plane but not pulled out sideways, and
                    that is the only reason a finished puzzle can be slid across a table in one piece.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Lose it and you have a grid of squares with bumps on — which looks perfectly convincing on screen
                    and comes apart in your hands. So the profile here is the classic seven-curve knob with a real
                    waist, every number in it a fraction of the edge so it is the same shape at any piece size, and
                    the difficulty control never touches the neck: <em>where</em> a knob sits and <em>how big</em> it
                    is are difficulty; whether it locks is not negotiable.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-5 grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The kerf is the fit</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The beam takes its own width out of every joint, so the pieces come out of the sheet about one
                        kerf loose. There is nothing to compensate — tightening the cut by half a kerf on each side
                        would weld each piece to its neighbour. What the tool can do is tell you, and suggest cutting
                        a 2 × 2 test before a 300-piece sheet: how loose "one kerf" feels depends on your material and
                        your focus, and it is the difference between a puzzle that clicks and one that slides apart.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Every joint, cut once</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A piece and its neighbour share an edge. Cutting each piece's own closed outline would send
                        the beam down every internal line twice: twice the job time, and a joint burnt a full extra
                        kerf loose. So the tool generates one line per shared edge, and the status bar counts joints
                        rather than pieces.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Glue the picture on first</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        A photo glued to the board and then cut with it comes apart cleanly along every joint. A photo
                        glued to loose pieces never lines up again — the kerf you just cut out is exactly the width of
                        the picture that is now missing. Print, mount, let it dry, then cut.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">What difficulty actually moves</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Three things at once, and it takes all three to make pieces that genuinely argue with each other:
                </p>
                <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-3">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">The corners wander</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            The lattice the pieces are cut on stops being a grid, so the pieces are different
                            <em>sizes</em> and the joints are no longer straight lines. This is the big one: a knob in
                            a different place is a detail, a piece of a different size is a different piece. Both
                            joints meeting at a corner read it from the same place, so however far it has moved they
                            still meet exactly.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Each knob slides</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Along its own edge, so two neighbouring joints do not carry their tabs at the same height
                            — which is what makes a row of pieces stop looking like a row.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Each knob is a different size</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Capped to a share of its own edge, so a knob on a corner that has wandered close to its
                            neighbour does not swallow the piece between them.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
                <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    At zero, every piece is the same shape. That is a genuinely handsome object — a perfect lattice —
                    and a completely broken puzzle: every piece fits every socket, so there is nothing to solve and no
                    way to know you are wrong. The tool says so in the status bar rather than leaving you to find out
                    after cutting.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                Pieces smaller than about 12 mm are hard on fingers and harder on knobs — at that size the necks are
                thin enough to snap off as the puzzle is broken up for the first time, which is exactly when nobody is
                being careful. Fewer, larger pieces make a better object than more, smaller ones.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
